<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manhattan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manhattan is an island at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> and the central borough of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City. Inhabited by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, it was bought from them in 1626, by tradition for goods worth 60 guilders, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-minuit">Peter Minuit</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, and became the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, capital of New Netherland. Taken by the English in 1664, it grew into the commercial and financial heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manhattan.jpg" alt="Manhattan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dllu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Empire_State_Building_from_Rockefeller_Center_New_York_City_dllu_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Manhattan is the geographically smallest of the five boroughs of New York City and is coextensive with New York County. It lies almost entirely on Manhattan Island, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-river">Hudson River</a> to the west and the East River to the east, while the Harlem River divides it from the Bronx. The island is 13.4 miles long and 2.3 miles wide at its widest point, near 14th Street, and is loosely divided into Lower Manhattan, Midtown and Upper Manhattan. Its bedrock of Inwood marble, Fordham gneiss and Manhattan schist is well suited to the foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skyscraper">skyscrapers</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Manhattan was part of Lenapehoking, the territory of the Munsee, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and Wappinger peoples. Their main trail, from what is now Inwood to Battery Park, later became Broadway. Giovanni da Verrazzano, sailing for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-of-france">Francis I of France</a>, became the first documented European to visit the area, in April 1524. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonists set up a trading post on the island in 1624, named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> in 1626. The English took the territory in 1664 and renamed it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> for the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-maine">York</a>.</p><p>Manhattan was at the centre of the New York Campaign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, and the Continental Army abandoned it after the Battle of Fort Washington on 16 November 1776. British occupation lasted until 25 November 1783. New York was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1785 until 1790, and by 1810 it was the most populous city in the country. The Commissioners' Plan of 1811 laid out the grid, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a> opened in 1825. The subway opened in 1904, and Manhattan became a borough in the consolidation of 1898.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Manhattan had 1,694,250 residents at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, in a land area of about 22.7 square miles. It is the economic and administrative centre of New York City, with City Hall, Wall Street, the New York Stock Exchange and the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in Turtle Bay. Central Park, designed by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux in the 1850s, covers 840 acres. The Statue of Liberty is part of the borough, and its landmarks include the Empire State Building, Grand Central Terminal and Times Square.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manhattan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Juan Rodriguez, born in Santo Domingo of Portuguese and African descent, wintered on <b>Manhattan</b> in 1613–1614 trading for the Dutch, the first recorded non-Native inhabitant of the future New York City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Shuffle Along, a musical of 1921 produced, composed, directed and written by African Americans, ran for 504 performances on Broadway; Langston Hughes later wrote that <b>Manhattan</b>’s Black Renaissance “began with Shuffle Along”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The male gospel group <b>Manhattan</b> Harmony Four recorded it in 1923 as &quot;Lift Every Voice and Sing (National Negro Anthem)&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lift-every-voice-and-sing">Lift Every Voice and Sing</a></li><li><blockquote>Mark David Chapman shot John Lennon outside the Dakota building in <b>Manhattan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-8">8 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dutch settlement on <b>Manhattan</b> was granted its own city government under Director-General Peter Stuyvesant.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The blast shattered windows across <b>Manhattan</b> and damaged the Statue of Liberty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
